sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
----
statement:

The straight line drawn at right angles to the diameter of a circle from its end will fall outside the circle, and into the space between the straight line and the circumference another straight line cannot be interposed, further the angle of the semicircle is greater, and the remaining angle less, than any acute rectilinear angle.
----
----
proof:

Let {ABC} be a circle about {D} as center and {AB} as diameter.

I say that the straight line drawn from {A} at right angles to {AB} from its end will fall outside the circle.

For suppose it does not, but, if possible, let it fall within as {CA}, and join {DC}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="380" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.16 diagram" src="propIII16.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Three arguments in one figure: the perpendicular at A falls outside
    // the circle (reductio on CA), no line fits between it and the
    // circumference (reductio on FA), and the two horn angles at A bound
    // every acute rectilinear angle. Joyce's figure carries all three at
    // once; the walk brings each piece on with its own sentence.
    //
    // Everything added below is fully transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0
    // arc and triangle), so the static figure is unchanged. DG is the
    // perpendicular the proof draws from D; Joyce draws only DH, the radius
    // along it (H lies between D and G), so DG renders gold only while its
    // stroke runs or a slide lights it. CHA is the arc of the circle from C
    // through H to A — the "circumference CHA" of the horn-angle argument,
    // which needs a name of its own so a slide can light it beside BA and
    // AE. It passes through H, so it tracks the figure however F is dragged.
    //
    // Angle markers: the right angles BAE (the hypothesis) and AGD (the
    // I.12 perpendicular) are red; DAC / ACD are the reductio pair, DAG the
    // acute angle at A in triangle AGD, FAE the rectilinear angle FA makes
    // with the tangent. Same-vertex markers at A nest by auto
    // radius-stepping (smallest span innermost). Angle BAF, the rectilinear
    // angle the third part compares with the angle of the semicircle, has
    // the same rays as DAG (D on AB, G on AF), so one marker serves both.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","AB","D","ABC"];
    var SUPP  = GIVEN.concat(["C","CA","CD"]);
    var ISOS  = SUPP.concat(["DA","angDAC","angACD"]);
    var TRI   = ISOS.concat(["ACD"]);
    // The first reductio is closed: its markers and triangle go, the
    // supposed line CA stays as Joyce drew it.
    var PART2 = SUPP.concat(["DA"]);
    var TANG  = PART2.concat(["E","AE","angBAE"]);
    var ARC   = TANG.concat(["CHA"]);
    var INTER = ARC.concat(["F","AF","G","DG"]);
    var ANGS  = INTER.concat(["angAGD","angDAG"]);
    var HPT   = ANGS.concat(["H","DH"]);
    // The second reductio is closed: the right angle at G goes, the
    // interposed line and the angle DAG (= BAF) stay for the third part.
    var PART3 = INTER.concat(["angDAG","H","DH"]);
    var HORN  = PART3.concat(["angFAE"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.16",
        pivot: "D",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;240,240",
            "B;point;free;240,30",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "D;point;midpoint;A,B;0,0,0;120,0,0",
            "ABC;circle;radius;D,A;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "C;point;circleSlider;ABC,120,130",
            "CA;line;connect;C,A",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "AE';line;perpendicular;A,D;0;0;0",
            "E;point;lineSlider;AE',30,220",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "F;point;free;60,110",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "G;point;foot;D,AF",
            "H;point;cutoff;D,G,D,A",
            "DH;line;connect;D,H",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — AB already draws DA, and DH
            // draws the part of DG inside the circle.
            "DA;line;connect;D,A;0;0;0",
            "DG;line;connect;D,G;0;0;0",
            "ACD;polygon;triangle;A,C,D;0;0;0;0",
            "CHA;sector;arc;C,H,A;0;0;0;0",
            "angDAC;sector;angleMarker;A,D,C",
            "angACD;sector;angleMarker;C,A,D",
            "angBAE;sector;angleMarker;A,B,E;0;0;red;red",
            "angAGD;sector;angleMarker;G,A,D;0;0;red;red",
            "angDAG;sector;angleMarker;A,D,G",
            "angFAE;sector;angleMarker;A,F,E"
        ],
        // C, E and F are draggable (a circleSlider, a lineSlider, a free
        // point — checked against the bundle), and draggables are
        // auto-unioned into every slide. Each is introduced mid-walk ("let
        // it fall within as CA", "let it fall as AE", "as FA"), so all three
        // are deferred and follow `visible`. A and B are the given diameter.
        // D (midpoint), G (foot) and H (cutoff) are derived, so they are
        // listed per slide instead. The resting figure is unaffected.
        deferDraggables: ["C","E","F"],
        // ACD is the triangle; the angle ACD takes the display override
        // {ACD|angACD} in prose and captions.
        aliases: { "AD": "DA", "DC": "CD", "AC": "CA", "BA": "AB", "EA": "AE",
                   "FA": "AF", "GD": "DG", "HD": "DH", "CDA": "ACD",
                   "DAC": "angDAC", "CAD": "angDAC", "DCA": "angACD",
                   "AGD": "angAGD", "DGA": "angAGD", "DAG": "angDAG",
                   "GAD": "angDAG", "BAE": "angBAE", "EAB": "angBAE",
                   "FAE": "angFAE", "EAF": "angFAE" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”), split at its seams.
            { text: "“The straight line drawn at right angles to the diameter of a circle from its end will fall outside the circle,”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [] },
            { text: "“and into the space between the straight line and the circumference another straight line cannot be interposed,”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [] },
            { text: "“further the angle of the semicircle is greater, and the remaining angle less, than any acute rectilinear angle.”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.16" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABC} be a circle about {D} as center and {AB} as diameter.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["ABC","D","AB"] },
            { text: "I say that the straight line drawn from {A} at right angles to {AB} from its end will fall outside the circle.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["A","AB"] },
            { text: "For suppose it does not, but, if possible, let it fall within as {CA}, and join {DC}.",
              visible: SUPP,
              highlighted: ["C", "CA", "CD"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "C",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "CA", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CD", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Since {DA} equals {DC}, the angle {DAC} also equals the angle {ACD|angACD}.",
              visible: ISOS,
              highlighted: ["DA","CD"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angDAC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angACD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "DA and DC are radii",                     ref: "I.Def.15" },
                  { claim: "angles at the base of an isosceles triangle", ref: "I.5" } ] },
            { text: "But the angle {DAC} is right, therefore the angle {ACD|angACD} is also right.",
              visible: ISOS,
              highlighted: ["angDAC","angACD"] },
            { text: "Thus, in the triangle {ACD}, the two angles {DAC} and {ACD|angACD} equal two right angles, which is impossible.",
              visible: TRI,
              highlighted: ["ACD","angDAC","angACD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "two angles of a triangle are less than two right angles", ref: "I.17" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the straight line drawn from the point {A} at right angles to {BA} will not fall within the circle.",
              visible: PART2,
              highlighted: ["A","AB"] },
            { text: "Similarly we can prove that neither will it fall on the circumference, therefore it will fall outside. Let it fall as {AE}.",
              visible: TANG,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "E",      name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "angBAE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "I say next that into the space between the straight line {AE} and the circumference {CHA} another straight line cannot be interposed.",
              visible: ARC,
              highlighted: ["AE","CHA"] },
            { text: "For, if possible, let another straight line be so interposed, as {FA}. Draw {DG} from the point {D} perpendicular to {FA}.",
              visible: INTER,
              highlighted: ["D"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "F",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "G",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "DG", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "DG perpendicular to FA", ref: "I.12" }] },
            { text: "Then, since the angle {AGD} is right, and the angle {DAG} is less than a right angle, {AD} is greater than {DG}.",
              visible: ANGS,
              highlighted: ["DA","DG"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angAGD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angDAG", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "two angles of a triangle are less than two right angles", ref: "I.17" },
                  { claim: "the greater angle is subtended by the greater side",       ref: "I.19" } ] },
            { text: "But {DA} equals {DH}, therefore {DH} is greater than {DG}, the less greater than the greater, which is impossible.",
              visible: HPT,
              highlighted: ["DA","DG"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "H",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "DH", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "DA and DH are radii", ref: "I.Def.15" }] },
            { text: "Therefore another straight line cannot be interposed into the space between the straight line and the circumference.",
              visible: PART3,
              highlighted: ["AE","CHA"] },
            { text: "I say further that the angle of the semicircle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA} is greater than any acute rectilinear angle, and the remaining angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE} is less than any acute rectilinear angle.",
              visible: PART3,
              highlighted: ["AB","CHA","AE"] },
            // His one long conditional is two slides: the supposition, then
            // what it would give.
            { text: "For, if there is any rectilinear angle greater than the angle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA}, and any rectilinear angle less than the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE},",
              visible: PART3,
              highlighted: ["AB","CHA","AE"] },
            { text: "then into the space between the circumference and the straight line {AE} a straight line will be interposed such as will make an angle contained by straight lines which is greater than the angle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA}, and another angle contained by straight lines which is less than the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE}.",
              visible: HORN,
              // FA is that line, and it makes the angles BAF (the marker DAG,
              // same rays) and FAE — the latter is this slide's animation
              // target.
              highlighted: ["AF","angDAG"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angFAE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "But such a straight line cannot be interposed, therefore there will not be any acute angle contained by straight lines which is greater than the angle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA}, nor yet any acute angle contained by straight lines which is less than the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE}.",
              visible: HORN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CHA","AE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "no straight line can be interposed", ref: "Above" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the straight line drawn at right angles to the diameter of a circle from its end will fall outside the circle, and into the space between the straight line and the circumference another straight line cannot be interposed, further the angle of the semicircle is greater, and the remaining angle less, than any acute rectilinear angle.",
              visible: HORN,
              highlighted: ["AE","CHA","AB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.16") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    // "Above" is deliberately unmapped: it renders as plain text, as it
    // does in the margin.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Def.15": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15/",
        "I.5":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI5/",
        "I.12":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI12/",
        "I.17":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI17/",
        "I.19":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI19/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since {DA} equals {DC}, the angle {DAC} also equals the angle {ACD|angACD}. [!just I.5]

But the angle {DAC} is right, therefore the angle {ACD|angACD} is also right.  Thus, in the triangle {ACD}, the two angles {DAC} and {ACD|angACD} equal two right angles, which is impossible. [!just I.17]

Therefore the straight line drawn from the point {A} at right angles to {BA} will not fall within the circle.

Similarly we can prove that neither will it fall on the circumference, therefore it will fall outside. Let it fall as {AE}.

I say next that into the space between the straight line {AE} and the circumference {CHA} another straight line cannot be interposed.

For, if possible, let another straight line be so interposed, as {FA}. Draw {DG} from the point {D} perpendicular to {FA}. [!just I.12]

Then, since the angle {AGD} is right, and the angle {DAG} is less than a right angle, {AD} is greater than {DG}. [!just I.17, I.19]

But {DA} equals {DH}, therefore {DH} is greater than {DG}, the less greater than the greater, which is impossible.

Therefore another straight line cannot be interposed into the space between the straight line and the circumference.

I say further that the angle of the semicircle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA} is greater than any acute rectilinear angle, and the remaining angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE} is less than any acute rectilinear angle.

For, if there is any rectilinear angle greater than the angle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA}, and any rectilinear angle less than the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE}, then into the space between the circumference and the straight line {AE} a straight line will be interposed such as will make an angle contained by straight lines which is greater than the angle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA}, and another angle contained by straight lines which is less than the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE}.

<div class="just">Above</div>

But such a straight line cannot be interposed, therefore there will not be any acute angle contained by straight lines which is greater than the angle contained by the straight line {BA} and the circumference {CHA}, nor yet any acute angle contained by straight lines which is less than the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the straight line {AE}.

Therefore *the straight line drawn at right angles to the diameter of a circle from its end will fall outside the circle, and into the space between the straight line and the circumference another straight line cannot be interposed, further the angle of the semicircle is greater, and the remaining angle less, than any acute rectilinear angle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
----
guide:

#### Horn angles

Back in Book I, @I.Def.8, Euclid defined an angle and included the possibility that the sides were not straight lines.  Those whose sides were straight were called *rectilinear angles* in @I.Def.9.  Until this proposition we only saw rectilinear angles.  In this proposition Euclid showed that the angle contained by the circumference {CHA} and the tangent straight line {AE} is less than any acute rectilinear angle.  An angle of that sort is commonly called a *horn angle*.  Its complement, the angle contained by the diameter and the circumference, is less than a right angle but greater than any acute rectilinear angle.

There's a lot that could be said about these curvilinear angles, but their only appearance in the *Elements* is in this proposition.

Horn angles are infinitesimal with respect to rectilinear angles, that is, no multiple of a horn angle is greater than any rectilinear angle, or equivalently, no part (meaning fraction) of a rectilinear angle is less than a horn angle.  The contemplation of horn angles leads to difficulties in the theory of proportions that's developed in [Book V](/elements/books/bookV/).

#### Use of Proposition 16 and its corollary

This proposition is used in the proof of proposition @IV.4 and two others in Book IV.  The corollary is used in @III.17, @III.33, @III.37, a few propositions in Book IV, and @XII.16.
#### prop_section ####
kind: corollary
----
toc_summary: From this it is manifest that the straight line drawn at right angles to the diameter of a circle from its end touches the circle.
----
----
proof: From this it is clear that the straight line drawn at right angles to the diameter of a circle from its end touches the circle.
